+**DECISION-279 — Site ingest gains a staged, content-addressed transport; the legacy single POST stays.**
+
+**Status:** Resolved (architect, Phase 2; tech-lead, Phase 3; built as designed, deviations recorded in the work-log) · **Date:** 2026-10-26 · **Feature:** `2026-10-26-site-ingest-size` (F324)
+
+- **The defect.** The CI ingest sent every image, base64, in one request. A real site's single request was 37.8 MB against Vercel's 4.5 MB function-body cap, which answers a plain-text 413 before any route code runs. Nothing in the route could raise the cap.
+- **Three fixed-path routes** beside the legacy one: `POST /api/sites/ingest/{plan,images,commit}`. They share one gate (`authorizeIngest`: OIDC, then the `sites.public_render` flag 503, then the repository's site 404, then `already_current`) and one publish function (`publishSiteBundle`: store the private bundle, `recordSiteIngest`, audit, revalidate) with the legacy route, through `src/lib/sites-ingest.ts`. The client picks legacy only when the whole bundle is at most 4,000,000 bytes, so neither repository's deploy order can break a small site.
+- **The server keeps no session state.** `blob_assets` is the staging area: a plan asks which sha256 digests the organization lacks as `public_site` images, an upload stores one image, a commit publishes. A re-run converges because the commit sha is written last, in the single `recordSiteIngest` UPDATE.
+- **The server derives every key.** `commit` resolves each declared `{ manifestKey, sha256 }` through the new `BlobStore.findByContentHashes({ organizationId, audience, contentHashes })` (DECISION-030; `audience` a required string literal per DECISION-277) and builds `imageKeys` itself. The client never sends or receives a blob uuid; no plan, upload or commit response contains one. A hash held only as `private`, or only by another organization, counts as not held. The server recomputes every upload's hash and sniffs its magic bytes, and stores the sniffed type.
+- **One app-enforced limit, 4,000,000 bytes per request body, on all four routes** (about 11% under 4.5 MB): a declared `Content-Length` over it is refused without reading, otherwise the bytes actually read are counted and the stream is cancelled at the limit. It is checked only after the gate, so an unauthenticated caller sees a 401, not a 413. A local server and a production one behave the same, so the limit is testable.
+- **The wire is pinned in both repositories** (presby `sites-ingest.test.ts` SI-4/SI-9, presby-site-kit `ingest.test.ts` KI-1) and driven end to end by the bridge run: `MAX_REQUEST_BYTES` 4,000,000 · `MAX_MANIFEST_ENTRIES` 2,000 · headers `x-presby-ingest-protocol: 1` and `x-presby-sha256` · error body `{ status: "error", code, error }` over a closed code set (legacy errors gain `code`, additively) · manifest keys 1 to 200 UTF-16 units with no control characters (`__proto__` is data, `imageKeys` is built with `Object.fromEntries`).
+- **Audit.** `SITE_CONTENT_INGESTED` stays one row per publish, on both transports, with `transport: "legacy" | "staged"` added. Uploads and plans write none (nothing is published until commit).
+- **No schema change, no dependency, no proxy change, no flag.** The paths are fixed because the proxy matcher excludes any path ending in an image extension (F151): a dynamic `/images/<key>.jpg` would have bypassed the custom-host lockdown.
+- **Implementation rulings.** Uploads are sequential, one image per request (revisit above about 500 images in a run or a run over five minutes). `storeSiteImage` retries a unique violation once, so identical concurrent uploads are safe (F289 Edge Case 17). The client throws `IngestFailure`, mints a fresh OIDC token for every request, retries a thrown fetch or any 5xx three times, and does not follow redirects. `ingest.yml` gains `transport: auto | staged | legacy` and a per-repository job concurrency group.
+- **Accepted residuals (`paper`).** Images uploaded by a run that never published stay as `public_site` rows no bundle uses; they can be fetched only by uuid, which no ingest response discloses, and nothing collects them (all deletes are soft; no takedown until F289 Tier 2). Re-running an older failed run after a newer one succeeded republishes the older content (the concurrency group serializes runs, it does not order commit shas). An image over 4,000,000 bytes cannot be published: the client stops before sending anything and names it. There is no per-organization storage budget. A page that references an image key the commit does not declare is not validated, in either transport. The upload path may first run in production at the first real `site-fpcw` publish.
+- **Rollout: the server before the client.** Promote this server, then merge the presby-site-kit change to its `main` (v4.1.0). Rollback of the client is a revert on presby-site-kit `main`; of the server, a revert of the release (a staged client then prints "the platform does not support staged ingest yet"; small sites are unaffected).
+
